    "['no shutdown', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set interfaces ge-0/0/0 disable', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"##\\nset interfaces ge-0/0/0 disable\\n##\\n\\nNote: In Juniper, `disable` is used to shut down an interface, so `no shutdown` in Cisco translates to removing the `disable` statement or setting it to `false`. However, since the request was for a direct equivalent of `no shutdown` (which enables the interface), the proper Juniper command would actually be:\\n```\\ndelete interfaces ge-0/0/0 disable\\n```\\nor\\n```\\nset interfaces ge-0/0/0 disable false\\n```\\nBut based on the exact instruction to provide the equivalent of `no shutdown`, the closest is `delete interfaces ge-0/0/0 disable`. \\n\\nThe initial response `set interfaces ge-0/0/0 disable` would actually shut down the interface (opposite of Cisco's `no shutdown`). Here's the corrected version:\\n\\n##\\ndelete interfaces ge-0/0/0 disable\\n##\\n\\nor alternatively:\\n\\n##\\nset interfaces ge-0/0/0 disable false\\n##\\n\\nThe first option (`delete`) is more idiomatic in Juniper for enabling an interface.\", 'trans_template': 'set interfaces ge-0/0/0 disable'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'delete interfaces ge-0/0/0 disable', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"##\\nset interfaces ge-0/0/0 disable\\n##\\n\\nNote: In Juniper, `disable` is used to shut down an interface, so `no shutdown` in Cisco translates to removing the `disable` statement or setting it to `false`. However, since the request was for a direct equivalent of `no shutdown` (which enables the interface), the proper Juniper command would actually be:\\n```\\ndelete interfaces ge-0/0/0 disable\\n```\\nor\\n```\\nset interfaces ge-0/0/0 disable false\\n```\\nBut based on the exact instruction to provide the equivalent of `no shutdown`, the closest is `delete interfaces ge-0/0/0 disable`. \\n\\nThe initial response `set interfaces ge-0/0/0 disable` would actually shut down the interface (opposite of Cisco's `no shutdown`). Here's the corrected version:\\n\\n##\\ndelete interfaces ge-0/0/0 disable\\n##\\n\\nor alternatively:\\n\\n##\\nset interfaces ge-0/0/0 disable false\\n##\\n\\nThe first option (`delete`) is more idiomatic in Juniper for enabling an interface.\", 'trans_template': 'delete interfaces ge-0/0/0 disable'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set interfaces ge-0/0/0 disable false', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"##\\nset interfaces ge-0/0/0 disable\\n##\\n\\nNote: In Juniper, `disable` is used to shut down an interface, so `no shutdown` in Cisco translates to removing the `disable` statement or setting it to `false`. However, since the request was for a direct equivalent of `no shutdown` (which enables the interface), the proper Juniper command would actually be:\\n```\\ndelete interfaces ge-0/0/0 disable\\n```\\nor\\n```\\nset interfaces ge-0/0/0 disable false\\n```\\nBut based on the exact instruction to provide the equivalent of `no shutdown`, the closest is `delete interfaces ge-0/0/0 disable`. \\n\\nThe initial response `set interfaces ge-0/0/0 disable` would actually shut down the interface (opposite of Cisco's `no shutdown`). Here's the corrected version:\\n\\n##\\ndelete interfaces ge-0/0/0 disable\\n##\\n\\nor alternatively:\\n\\n##\\nset interfaces ge-0/0/0 disable false\\n##\\n\\nThe first option (`delete`) is more idiomatic in Juniper for enabling an interface.\", 'trans_template': 'set interfaces ge-0/0/0 disable false'}]]": 1,
